WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.833
Scikit-learn.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:07.667
It's a classical machine learning library, but the lasting lesson is its API contract.

3
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:11.500
Every scikit-learn object follows one rhythm.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.267
Estimators learn with fit.

5
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:14.967
They answer with predict.

6
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.922
And preprocessors reshape data with transform.

7
00:00:18.922 --> 00:00:24.167
XGBoost, LightGBM, and many wrappers imitate this exact interface.

8
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:29.700
A Pipeline chains preprocessing and a model into a single estimator.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.700
Training data goes in first.

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.467
The scaler is fitted on that training data only.

11
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.300
The model then learns from the scaled features.

12
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.467
Unseen data flows through the same fitted steps.

13
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.279
This quietly fixes the classic leakage bug.

14
00:00:44.279 --> 00:00:49.200
If you scale before splitting, information from the test set leaks into training.

15
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:56.000
With a pipeline, the scaler only ever sees training data, automatically, even inside cross-validation.

16
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.733
Here's the full thing in code.

17
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:02.067
First we hold out twenty percent of the data for testing.

18
00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:06.433
Then we chain a scaler and a logistic regression into one pipeline.

19
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:11.467
One fit call trains the whole chain, and score evaluates it on the held-out data.

20
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.367
Here's why a baseline matters.

21
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:22.051
Before the light-curve CNN exists, your exoplanet classifier is exactly this pipeline over tabular features.

22
00:01:22.051 --> 00:01:25.333
It scores zero point eight nine in an afternoon.

23
00:01:25.633 --> 00:01:31.546
When the CNN reaches zero point nine one, you know the extra complexity earns its keep.

24
00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:37.867
Both expose fit and predict style interfaces, so your eval harness runs them interchangeably.

25
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:39.826
Let's recap.

26
00:01:39.826 --> 00:01:42.800
Fit, predict, transform: learn the rhythm once.

27
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.533
Many other tools imitate this interface.

28
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:47.533
A pipeline prevents leakage.

29
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:49.965
And always build a baseline first.

30
00:01:49.965 --> 00:01:52.033
That wraps up Python foundations.
